  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA bit of uncertainty exists regarding the long term forecast. \nEnsemble guidance is consistent on southwesterly flow overhead at \n500-mb Monday morning, with a deep upper trough across the Western \nUnited States. The trough is expected to move eastward across the \nCentral and Southern United States through the duration of the long \nterm period. However, the eastward speed of the trough is highly in \nquestion. In general, warm conditions are forecast ahead of the \ntrough, with cooler and wetter conditions along a cold front as the \ntrough moves overhead. Once the trough fully moves through the area, \nwarmer and drier conditions may be allowed to return to the area. \n\nEnsembles show the trough beginning to move overhead sometime \nbetween Monday evening and Tuesday morning. However, they also show \nlarge amounts of uncertainty centered around the trailing edge of \nthe trough. This would indicate a slower progression of this trough \neastward. Around 1 in 3 LREF members show the trough still lingering \nin Southern California by midnight Tuesday, whereas the other \nmembers show the trough moving out or already moved out of that \narea. If the slower scenario occurs, warmer temperatures may hang \naround into Tuesday. However, highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s \nare forecast only on Monday. \n\nAs the trough moves over the area, a cold front is expected to \ntraverse the area, bringing cooler temperatures and precipitation to \nthe area. As this process is favored to occur sometime before \nTuesday afternoon, highs are forecast in the upper 60s to mid 70s \nacross the forecast area Tuesday. Additionally, NBM guidance \nsuggests a 50-70% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of rain across \nthe entire forecast area as the front comes through the area. \nCurrently, thunderstorms are not favored along this frontal \nboundary, as LREF guidance suggests less than a 10% chance that \nsurface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) exceeds 1000 \nJ/kg. The exception is Monday evening, where areas along and east of \nU.S. Highway 83 show as high as a 1 in 3 chance that CAPE exceeds \n1000 J/kg. However, the front would need to come through Monday \nevening in order to take advantage of this atmospheric instability \nand produce thunderstorms. Otherwise, widespread rain seems more \nlikely as the front comes through. \n\nConditions are expected to remain cool and wet as the trough remains \nin the area. Most LREF members begin showing the trough moving out \nof the area around midday Wednesday, but may not occur until as late \nas Thursday. Once the trough fully moves through the area, \nconditions are expected to become warmer and drier. NBM \nprobabilities for 24 hr precipitation greater than 0.1 inches begin \nto decrease Thursday (down to about 1 in 4 chance), as upper \npercentile highs (90th and above) show 80s reaching back into some \nareas of the forecast region. \n\nIt should be noted that the slowest progressions of the trough could \ncreate a potential for flash flooding with rainfall. Most recent EC \ndeterministics even show the trough stalling out in the 4-Corners \nRegion Wednesday evening, and remaining in place into Friday. NBM 48 \nhr precipitation guidance shows most of the region at a 30-40% \nchance for more than an inch of rain across Tuesday and Wednesday, \nwith high end scenarios (90th and above) for 24 hr precipitation \nshowing totals between 1 and 2.5 inches Tuesday through Thursday. \nThis is a low confidence scenario (about 5%), but could potentially \nhave notable flash flooding impacts if it occurs.",
